   111	- **Algorithm.** There is a Las Vegas algorithm. In expected time (M_rows)^{2}·(log N)^{O_d(1)}, it outputs a
   112	  nonempty set S of rows and Y ∈ (Z/N)^× with X_S² ≡ Y² (mod N), where X_S = ∏_{i∈S} r_i.
   113	- **Invariance.** S and Y are functions of the certificates, the field and the algorithm's own coins only. So the
   114	  exposed transcript satisfies Theorem DD's hypothesis (T).
   115	- **Consequence.** Pr[1 < gcd(X_S − Y, N) < N] = 1 − 2^{1−ω(N)} ≥ 1/2 (lemmaM Theorem DD).
   116	
   117	**Algorithm LV6.**
   118	1. *Columns* (§3.1). The columns are: the sign of q_i; for each prime p ≤ B, the parity of v_p(q_i); one parity
   119	   column E_i mod 2; for each prime r ≤ B with r ∤ D and each root s of f mod r, the parity of
   120	   Σ_P e_{iP}·[a_P ≡ b_P s mod r]·v_r(F(a_P, b_P)); and t_χ character columns (§3.3).
   121	2. *Characters* (§3.3). Compute x from the explicit discriminant bound. Sample t_χ first-degree primes (r_j, s_j)
   122	   of K independently and uniformly among those with norm in (x/2, x]. Column j holds
   123	   Σ_P e_{iP}·χ_j(a_P − b_P α) mod 2, where χ_j(a − bα) = ((a − b s_j)/r_j).
   124	3. *Linear algebra* (§3.4). Sample two left-kernel vectors s₁, s₂ of the M_rows × C_col matrix over F₂, verify
   125	   them, and retry until they are linearly independent.
   126	4. *Square test* (§3.5). For S ∈ (s₁, s₂, s₁ + s₂), in this order, run Algorithm SQ on
   127	   Γ_S := g′(β)²·∏_P z_P^{|e_P(S)|}. Take the first S for which it returns η ∈ Z[β] with η² = Γ_S.
   128	5. *Congruence* (§3.6). Set Y := √q_S · t^{−E(S)/2} · ψ(η) · (ψ(γ⁻_S)·ψ(g′(β)))^{−1} (mod N), where
   129	   q_S = ∏_{i∈S} q_i, √q_S is computed by halving exponents, and γ⁻_S := ∏_{e_P(S)<0} z_P^{|e_P(S)|}.
   130	6. If step 3 or step 4 fails (probability ≤ 2/(log N)² + 2^{−9} per attempt; §3.3, §3.4), resample the characters
   131	   and kernel vectors.
