   135	## 3. Proof
   136	
   137	### 3.1 Columns: Lemma C (good primes)
   138	
   139	**Lemma C [proved].** Let r be a prime with r ∤ D, and let (a, b) be coprime with F(a, b) ≠ 0. Then:
   140	- (i) if r | b, then r ∤ F(a, b), and a − bα is a unit at every prime of O_K above r;
   141	- (ii) if r ∤ b, the only prime 𝔭 of O_K above r with ord_𝔭(a − bα) ≠ 0 is the first-degree prime
   142	  𝔭 = (r, α − ab^{−1}). It occurs only if r | F(a, b), and then ord_𝔭(a − bα) = v_r(F(a, b)).
   143	
   144	*Proof.*
   145	- Since r ∤ t, α is r-integral. Z_(r)[α] has discriminant disc(f)/t^{2d−2} up to an r-unit, which is prime to r.
   146	  So Z_(r)[α] is the maximal order at r, and by Dedekind–Kummer the primes above r are (r, φ_j(α)), one for each
   147	  irreducible factor φ_j of f mod r, all unramified.
   148	- (i) If r | b, then r ∤ a, and F(a, b) ≡ t a^d ≢ 0 (mod r). Also a − bα ≡ a (mod 𝔭) for every 𝔭 | r.
   149	- (ii) a − bα ∈ (r, φ_j(α)) iff φ_j(ab^{−1}) ≡ 0, i.e. iff φ_j = x − ab^{−1}, which is a first-degree prime 𝔭.
   150	  - Norm_{K/Q}(a − bα) = F(a, b)/t, and 𝔭 is the only prime above r dividing a − bα.
   151	  - Since f(𝔭/r) = 1, ord_𝔭(a − bα) = v_r(F(a, b)/t) = v_r(F(a, b)). ∎
   152	- Primes r > B with r ∤ D contribute nothing: r ∤ F(a, b) because F(a, b) is B-smooth.
   153	- Hence, for any S whose algebraic (r, s)-columns sum to zero mod 2:
   154	  ord_𝔓(γ_S) ≡ 0 (mod 2) for every prime 𝔓 of O_K not above a prime dividing D, where γ_S := ∏_P (a_P − b_Pα)^{e_P(S)}.
   155	- Bad primes need no columns. Neither the index [O_K : Z[β]] nor the factorization of D is ever computed.
   156	- (Tested: `test_good_prime_columns_lemma_C`.)
   157	
   158	**Rational side.** If the sign, the p-parity (p ≤ B) and the E-parity columns of S sum to zero, then
   159	q_S = g₁^{E(S)}·∏_P n_P^{−e_P(S)} (sign +1) is a square in Q^×, with explicit square root
   160	√q_S = g₁^{E/2} ∏_{p≤B} p^{−(Σ_P e_P v_p(n_P))/2}.
   161	- The **one** parity column also gives E(S) even, which makes t^{−E(S)} a square.
   162	- So γ_S = t^{−E(S)}·∏_P z_P^{e_P(S)} is a square in K iff γ′_S := ∏_P z_P^{e_P(S)} is.
   163	- This is the "g₁ column" of pcest §5. It counts atomic relations with multiplicity, and it handles f_d as well.
   164	
   165	### 3.2 The residual square-class group: Lemma H
   166	
   167	Let S_D be the set of primes of O_K above rational primes dividing D. Define
   168	  V_D := {γ ∈ K^× : ord_𝔓(γ) even for all 𝔓 ∉ S_D},   H := V_D/K^{×2}.
   169	By §3.1, every S in the kernel of the non-character columns has γ_S ∈ V_D. Write φ(S) := [γ_S] ∈ H. Then φ is
   170	F₂-linear on the kernel, because γ_{S+S′} = γ_Sγ_{S′}/γ_{S∩S′}².
   171	
   172	**Lemma H [proved; standard].** |H| ≤ 2^{|S_D|}·|Cl(K)[2]|·2^{r₁+r₂} ≤ 2^{d·ω(D) + d}·h_K. With the bound of
   173	Lenstra (Bull. AMS 26 (1992), Thm 6.5) applied to M_K ≤ √|Δ_K| ≤ √|disc g|, this gives
   174	  log₂|H| ≤ d·log₂|D| + d + ½log₂|disc g| + (d−1)·log₂(d + log₂|disc g|) = O_d(log N).
   175	*Proof.*
   176	- Put W := {γ : γO_K = 𝔞²} and Y := O_K^×K^{×2}. Then V_D ⊃ W ⊃ Y ⊃ K^{×2}.
   177	- The map V_D → ⊕_{𝔓∈S_D} Z/2, γ ↦ (ord_𝔓 γ mod 2), has kernel W.
   178	- The map W → Cl(K)[2], γ ↦ [𝔞], is well defined (the ideal group is torsion-free) and has kernel Y.
   179	- Y/K^{×2} ≅ O_K^×/O_K^{×2} has order 2^{r₁+r₂} ≤ 2^d (Dirichlet; roots of unity ±1 for odd d).
   180	- |S_D| ≤ d·ω(D) ≤ d·log₂|D|.
   181	- Sizes. ‖f‖_∞ ≤ C_d·g₁H = N^{O(1)}, so |disc f| ≤ d^{2d}‖f‖^{2d−2} and log|D|, log|disc g| = O_d(log N). ∎
   182	- **Compared with LV (Claim 6.5).**
   183	  - LV use BLP's [V : W] ≤ [O_K : Z[f_dα]]. We use the cruder 2^{|S_D|}, which needs no l-functions on the
   184	    order, no projective-root bookkeeping, and no index computation.
   185	  - Units, class-group 2-torsion, the index and the non-monic leading coefficient are all inside H. None of them
   186	    is assumed trivial.
   187	  - The cost is a constant factor in the dimension: O(log N) either way.
   188	- (`numfield.class_group_log2_bound` computes this bound; E1 reports 271–604 bits for the toy fields.)
