   198	- At 𝔓 | D, 𝔓 ∤ 2: ord_𝔓 𝔡 ≤ 1. At 𝔓 | 2: ord_𝔓 𝔡 ≤ 2·ord_𝔓(2) + 1.
   199	- So N(𝔡) ≤ 8^d·∏_{𝔓|D} N𝔓 ≤ 8^d |D|^d, and |Δ_K| ≤ |disc g|. Then log Δ̄ = O_d(log N).
   200	- No small representative of h is needed. LV's minimal-norm representative argument is not used.
   201	
   202	**Exceptional classes.** Let c_L > 0 be the absolute constant in Landau's theorem below. Put
   203	  ν₁ := min( 1/(4·(2d)!·log Δ̄), c_L/(2 log Δ̄) ),
   204	and let E := {0 ≠ h ∈ H : ζ_{L_h} has a real zero β with 1 − ν₁ ≤ β < 1}.
   205	
   206	**Lemma E [proved from cited results]. |E| ≤ 1, for every field.**
   207	- **Cited inputs.**
   208	  - Stark (Invent. Math. 23 (1974), Lemma 8): if a number field L has a real zero β of ζ_L with
   209	    1 − (c(L) log|Δ_L|)^{−1} ≤ β < 1, where c(L) = 4[L:Q]! for non-normal L, then ζ_F(β) = 0 for some quadratic
   210	    F ⊂ L.
   211	  - Landau (1918; Davenport, *Multiplicative Number Theory*, Ch. 14; LV Fact 6.24): there is an effective absolute
   212	    c_L > 0 such that, for distinct real primitive characters χ₁, χ₂ of conductors q₁, q₂, L(s, χ₁)L(s, χ₂) has at
   213	    most one real zero in (1 − c_L/log(q₁q₂), 1).
   214	- *Proof.*
   215	  - Let h ∈ E with zero β. Since ν₁ ≤ 1/(4(2d)! log Δ_{L_h}), Stark gives a quadratic F_h ⊂ L_h with
   216	    ζ_{F_h}(β) = ζ(β)L(β, χ_{F_h}) = 0. Since ζ(β) ≠ 0 on (0, 1), L(β, χ_{F_h}) = 0.
   217	  - [L_h : Q] = 2d with d odd, so L_h has at most one quadratic subfield: two would generate a biquadratic subfield
   218	    of degree 4 ∤ 2d. K has none, so L_h = K·F_h.
   219	  - h ↦ L_h is injective (Kummer theory), hence so is h ↦ F_h.
   220	  - |Δ_{F_h}| ≤ |Δ_{L_h}|^{1/d} ≤ Δ̄. Two distinct h, h′ ∈ E would give distinct real primitive characters with
   221	    zeros ≥ 1 − ν₁ ≥ 1 − c_L/log(|Δ_{F_h}||Δ_{F_{h′}}|). Landau forbids this. ∎
   222	  - (Codex lv6_1 found the bound |E| ≤ 1. LV's dyadic-block argument gives O(log log N), plus an exceptional set
   223	    of fields. Neither is needed at fixed d.)
   224	
   225	**Sampler.**
   226	- Let log x := ⌈max(20d·(log Δ̄)², (log N)²)⌉.
   227	- Draw r uniform in (x/2, x]. If r is prime (AKS), compute the set Z_r of simple roots of f mod r (Las Vegas root
   228	  finding). With probability |Z_r|/d, output (r, s) with s uniform in Z_r; otherwise redraw.
   229	- The output is uniform over first-degree primes 𝔭 = (r, α − s) of K with N𝔭 ∈ (x/2, x]. All of these have
   230	  r > Δ̄ ≥ |D| and r > B.
   231	- The expected number of draws is O(d log x). Each draw costs (log x)^{O(1)} = (log N)^{O(1)}.
   232	
   233	**Lemma X [proved from cited results].** For every field and every h ∈ H ∖ (E ∪ {0}),
   234	  Pr_𝔭[χ_𝔭(h) = −1] ≥ 1/(5d)  (N ≥ N₀(d)).
   235	Consequently, with t_χ := ⌈5d·(ln |H|_bound + 2 ln log N)⌉ independent characters,
   236	  Pr[H ∩ ⋂_j ker χ_j ⊄ E ∪ {0}] ≤ (log N)^{−2},
   237	and on the complement H₀ := H ∩ ⋂_j ker χ_j is a subgroup of order ≤ 2.
   238	- **Cited input.** Lagarias–Odlyzko (in *Algebraic Number Fields*, Durham 1975, Academic Press 1977, Thm 1.3).
   239	  - Setting: L/K Galois with group G, C a conjugacy class, and x ≥ exp(10 n_L (log d_L)²).
   240	  - Statement: |π_C(x) − (|C|/|G|)Li(x)| ≤ (|C|/|G|)Li(x^{β₀}) + c₁x·exp(−c₂ n_L^{−1/2}(log x)^{1/2}), with c₁, c₂
   241	    absolute and effective.
   242	  - The β₀ term is present only if ζ_L has a real zero β₀ ∈ [1 − (4 log d_L)^{−1}, 1).
   243	- *Proof.*
   244	  - **Setup.**
   245	    - Apply LO to L_h/K, G = C₂, C = {σ}. Here n_L = 2d, d_L ≤ Δ̄, and log x ≥ 20d(log Δ̄)².
   246	    - For 𝔭 ∤ 2D unramified, χ_𝔭(h) = −1 iff Frob_𝔭 = σ. The value is the Legendre symbol of a 𝔭-unit
   247	      representative; for atomic products it is ∏_P ((a_P − b_P s)/r)^{e_P}.
   248	  - **Lower bound on inert first-degree primes with norm in (x/2, x].**
   249	    - Main term: ½(Li(x) − Li(x/2)) ≥ x/(4 log x).
   250	    - The β₀ term, if present, has β₀ < 1 − ν₁ because h ∉ E. It is at most 2x^{1−ν₁} ≤ 2x·exp(−ν₁(log N)²), which
   251	      is o(x/log x) since ν₁ ≥ c_d/log N.
   252	    - The error term is 2c₁x·exp(−c₂ log N/√(2d)) = o(x/log x).
   253	    - Primes of degree ≥ 2 number ≤ d√x, and ramified primes ≤ log₂ Δ̄. Both are negligible.
   254	    - Total: ≥ x/(4 log x)·(1 − o(1)).
   255	  - **Upper bound on all first-degree primes with norm in (x/2, x]:** ≤ d(π(x) − π(x/2)) ≤ 1.1·d·x/log x.
   256	  - The ratio is ≥ 1/(5d).
   257	  - **The kernel.** Independence gives Pr[h ∈ ⋂ ker χ_j] ≤ (1 − 1/(5d))^{t_χ} for each nonexceptional h ≠ 0. A
   258	    union bound over |H| classes gives the stated probability.
   259	  - **The group.** H₀ is a subgroup contained in E ∪ {0}, so |H₀| ≤ 2. ∎
   260	
   261	**Cost.** t_χ = O_d(log N) characters. Each is sampled in (log N)^{O(1)} expected time and evaluated on all atoms in
   262	M_rows·k·(log N)^{O(1)}.
   263	- Under GRH, characters at primes of size (log N)^{O(1)} would do (cf. LV Claim 6.21). We do not use GRH.
   264	
   265	### 3.4 Linear algebra
   266	
   267	- The matrix has M_rows rows and C_col ≤ M_rows − 12 columns. Its density is w ≤ M_rows·(2k·log₂N + t_χ + 2)
   268	  nonzero entries.
   269	- A uniformly random left-kernel vector is obtained by Wiedemann's method:
   270	  - It runs over F_{2^e} with 2^e ≥ M_rows⁴ and is applied to the transpose.
   271	  - A singular-system solver with preconditioning is analysed rigorously in Wiedemann (IEEE Trans. IT 32 (1986)
   272	    54–62) and Kaltofen–Saunders (AAECC-9, LNCS 539 (1991), Thms 2–4). LP 1992 §10 uses the same method.
   273	  - "x − A⁻(Ax)" gives a uniform element of the kernel, given the solver's coins. Coordinate expansion over an F₂
   274	    basis of F_{2^e} returns an F₂ kernel vector.
   275	- Each kernel vector is verified, so the method is Las Vegas.
   276	- Expected cost O(M_rows·w + M_rows²)·(log N)^{O(1)} = (M_rows)^{2}(log N)^{O(1)}.
   277	- dim ker ≥ 12. So two uniform samples are independent with probability ≥ 1 − 2^{−11}, and the third test vector
   278	  s₁ + s₂ costs nothing.
   279	- **Why three tests suffice [proved].** φ(s₁), φ(s₂) ∈ H₀, and |H₀| ≤ 2. Either one of them is 0, or both equal the
   280	  unique nonzero element, and then φ(s₁ + s₂) = 0. In every case some S ∈ {s₁, s₂, s₁ + s₂} has γ′_S ∈ K^{×2},
   281	  and S ≠ ∅.
   282	
   283	### 3.5 Square root in K: Algorithm SQ and Lemma SQ
   284	
   285	**Algorithm SQ.**
   286	- **Input.**
   287	  - Γ ∈ Z[β], given as g′(β)² times a product of atoms z_P (with multiplicities).
   288	  - An integer A with this property: if Γ = η² for some η ∈ Z[β], then ‖η‖_∞ ≤ A.
   289	- **Steps.**
   290	  1. Let p be the least prime > B with p ∤ D. It is found among the O(log N) primes after B, since ω(D) = O(log N).
   291	     Then g mod p is squarefree, and Γ is a unit at p, because Norm(Γ) = ±disc(g)²·∏(t^{d−1}F(a_P, b_P))^{|e_P|}
   292	     has only prime factors ≤ B or dividing D.
   293	  2. Factor ḡ = φ₁⋯φ_τ over F_p (τ ≤ d), Las Vegas. In each F_p[y]/φ_j, test whether Γ is a square (Euler) and take
   294	     a square root ρ_j (Tonelli–Shanks in F_{p^{deg φ_j}}, Las Vegas). If some component is a non-square, output
   295	     NONSQUARE.
   296	  3. For each of the 2^{τ−1} sign vectors ε with ε₁ = +1:
   297	     - CRT the values ε_jρ_j to ρ_ε ∈ Z[β]/p.
   298	     - Newton-lift the inverse square root, z ↦ z + z(1 − Γz²)/2 mod p^{2^i}, to the first precision p^e > 2A.
   299	     - Put η_ε := Γz mod p^e with centred coefficients. If η_ε² = Γ exactly, output η_ε.
   300	  4. Output NONSQUARE.
   301	
   302	**Lemma SQ [proved].**
   303	- (i) **Correctness.**
   304	  - Any output η satisfies η² = Γ exactly.
   305	  - If Γ ∈ K^{×2}, then Γ = η² with η ∈ Z[β], and SQ outputs ±η.
   306	- (ii) **The bound A.** Let Mh := ⌊‖g‖₂⌋ + 1 (≥ Mahler(g) ≥ max_k max(1, |β_k|)) and g_d := d‖g‖₁Mh^{d−1}. Then
   307	  A := d·⌈U^{1/2}⌉·2^{d−1}Mh·g_d^{d−1}, U := g_d²·∏_P (‖z_P‖₁Mh^{d−1})^{|e_P|},
   308	  is valid. log₂A ≤ ½·Σ_P|e_P|·log₂(‖z_P‖₁Mh^{d−1}) + O(d² log(d‖g‖₁Mh)) = O_d(Σ|e_P|·log N).
   309	- (iii) **Cost.** The cost is O_d(𝖬(log A)·log log A) + (log N)^{O(1)} bit operations, where 𝖬 is integer
   310	  multiplication time. This is quasi-linear in the size of Γ.
   311	
   312	*Proof.*
   313	- **(i).**
   314	  - Write Γ = g′(β)²·γ with γ := ∏ z_P^{|e_P|} ∈ Z[β].
   315	  - If Γ = δ², δ ∈ K, then (δ/g′(β))² = γ ∈ O_K, so δ/g′(β) ∈ O_K.
   316	  - g′(β)O_K ⊂ Z[β]: indeed O_K ⊂ O_K^∨ ⊂ Z[β]^∨ = g′(β)^{−1}Z[β] (Euler's lemma; Z[β] ⊂ O_K). Hence
   317	    η := δ ∈ Z[β].
   318	  - p ∤ disc(g), so Z_p[β] ≅ ∏_j Z_p[y]/(G_j) is a product of unramified local rings, with G_j the Hensel lifts of
   319	    the φ_j.
   320	  - η mod p equals ±ρ_ε for exactly one class ε with ε₁ = +1.
   321	  - For p odd and Γ a unit, a unit square root with prescribed residue lifts uniquely (Hensel). So
   322	    η_ε ≡ ±η (mod p^e). Since p^e > 2A ≥ 2‖η‖_∞, centring recovers ±η exactly.
   323	  - Conversely, the check η_ε² = Γ is exact. NONSQUARE at step 2 is correct because η ∈ O_K would reduce to a
   324	    square in O_K/𝔓 = F_p[y]/φ_j (p is prime to the index).
   325	- **(ii).**
   326	  - |σ_k(z)| ≤ ‖z‖₁Mh^{d−1} and |g′(β_k)| ≤ g_d, so |σ_k(η)|² ≤ U.
   327	  - Coefficients come from the inverse Vandermonde: (V^{−1})_{jk} = [y^j](g(y)/(y − β_k))/g′(β_k).
   328	    - The numerator coefficients are ≤ 2^{d−1}Mh.
   329	    - ∏_k |g′(β_k)| = |disc g| ≥ 1 gives |g′(β_k)| ≥ g_d^{−(d−1)}.
   330	- **(iii).**
   331	  - Γ is computed by a product tree.
   332	  - Newton doubles the precision. Each step is O(d²) multiplications of ≤ log₂(p^e) = O(log A) bits.
   333	  - The sign enumeration multiplies by 2^{d−1} = O(1). ∎
   334	- (E1: all 92 NONSQUARE outputs were independently certified, and all 116 squares were verified. The ratio
   335	  log₂‖η‖/log₂A was 0.08–0.22, so A is conservative but polynomially tight.)
   336	- **Prior methods.**
   337	  - Couveignes (LNM 1554, 1993) and Thomé (2012) need a prime inert in K, or a sign fixed by the norm for odd d.
   338	  - Montgomery's and Nguyen's methods are lattice-based and heuristic.
   339	  - The 2^{τ−1}-branch enumeration avoids both issues, and is affordable because d is fixed.
   340	  - LV cite Montgomery "in time O(M²)" without a rigorous analysis. Our Lemma SQ replaces that step.
   341	
   342	### 3.6 The congruence and ψ
   343	
   344	- Let S be the chosen dependency, e_P := e_P(S), E := E(S) (even), and γ⁻ := ∏_{e_P<0} z_P^{|e_P|}.
   345	- ψ is a ring homomorphism on Z[β], so ψ(η)² = ψ(Γ_S) = ψ(g′(β))²·∏_P ψ(z_P)^{|e_P|}.
   346	- Hence, with Y as in step 5,
   347	  Y² = q_S·t^{−E}·∏_P ψ(z_P)^{|e_P|}/ψ(γ⁻)² = q_S·t^{−E}·∏_P ψ(z_P)^{e_P} ≡ ∏_{i∈S} r_i² (mod N) by (1.1).
   348	- **Units.**
   349	  - ψ(z_P) = tG(P)/g₁ is a unit: G(P) = σℓn, with n B-smooth, and ℓ ∤ N, otherwise a factor was found.
   350	  - ψ(g′(β)) = t^{d−2}f′(g₀/g₁) is a unit by the field check (§4).
   351	  - √q_S involves only g₁ and primes ≤ B.
   352	- No map on K^× is needed. Every identity is a ring identity in Z[β] or in Q, followed by reduction mod N.
   353	- E1 checks this exactly: 0 violations in 116 cases.
   354	- **Final rational square root.** √q_S is computed from the recorded exponent vectors (halving), with
   355	  M_rows·k·(log N)^{O(1)} operations. No square root mod N is ever extracted.
   356	
   357	### 3.7 Transcript invariance (T)
   358	
   359	Theorem DD needs the exposed transcript 𝒯 to be invariant under r_i ↦ ε_ir_i. Every quantity computed by LV6 is
   360	a function of (field, R_i, splits, atoms, certificates) and fresh coins:
   361	- the columns, the characters (r_j, s_j), the kernel vectors, the Hensel prime p, the branch ε, η, the choice
   362	  among s₁, s₂, s₁ + s₂, and Y.
   363	
   364	Field rejections (§4) depend on the field only. None of these reads r_i, a Jacobi symbol of r_i, or any gcd
   365	outcome. So (T) holds, and DD gives Pr[nontrivial | 𝒯] = 1 − 2^{1−ω}.
   366	- **Retry policy.**
   367	  - After a trivial gcd we restart with fresh rows, as in lemmaM's remark R1.
   368	  - (Optional refinement.) Reusing the rows with a new dependency S′ is also exact, provided S′ ∉ {0, S}:
   369	    conditioned on the outcome for S, ∏_{S′}ε_i is still uniform on μ₂. But a policy that adapts the *choice* of
   370	    S′ must keep S′ outside the span of all previously tested S. We do not need this.
   371	
   372	### 3.8 Cost and the five requirements (a)–(e)
   373	
   374	| item | where | cost / status |
   375	|---|---|---|
   376	| (a) heights V = N^{1/(4k)}L^{O(1)} | only in log A = O(k·M_rows·log N) | Lemma SQ quasi-linear; no box enumeration in post-processing |
   377	| (b) non-monic (f, g₁x − g₀) | β = f_dα, monic g, ψ(β) = f_d g₀/g₁; bad primes in H | Lemmas C, H, SQ |
   378	| (c) the g₁/f_d parity column | one column E_i mod 2 | §3.1 |
   379	| (d) (rows)^{2+o(1)} | Wiedemann L^{2a+o(1)}; characters, SQ (3 calls), √q_S at L^{a+o(1)} | §3.3–§3.6 |
   380	| (e) transcript invariance (T) | all steps are functions of certificates and coins | §3.7 |
   381	
   382	## 4. Field-level events, Lemma IR, and what changes in pcest
   383	
   384	The kept field of hypD Corollary K has law ∝ q_A, which amplifies the probability of a field event by at most
   385	1/μ, with μ ≥ (γ/2)^{2k+1}L^{−o(1)} = L^{−o(1)}. We instead **reject** bad fields before any descent attempt. The
   386	field law then becomes 𝒜 conditioned on the good event 𝒢. Since g_A ≤ 1, E[g_A | 𝒢] ≥ γ − Pr[𝒢ᶜ] ≥ γ/2. So D_k
   387	survives with γ/2, and FM, Theorem R, Lemma Kern, Prop 4.2 and Theorem 4.3, all stated for 𝒜, need **no change**.
   388	
   389	| event | bound | treatment |
   390	|---|---|---|
   391	| gcd(g₁, N) ≠ 1 | — | proper factor (0 < g₁ < N) |
   392	| degree drop f_d = 0 | **impossible**: f_d ≡ a_d ≡ N g₀^{−d} ≢ 0 (mod g₁) (codex) | — |
   393	| gcd(f_d, N) ≠ 1 | — | proper factor (0 < \|f_d\| < N) |
   394	| content(f) > 1 | content divides F(g₀, g₁) = N and is < N | proper factor |
   395	| f reducible | ≤ exp(−log H/(13d log log H)) (Lemma IR) | reject; a factorization F₁F₂ = N with both ≠ ±1 is output |
   396	| N \| f′(g₀/g₁) | ≤ 1/(2H+1) | reject (a proper gcd is a factor) |
   397	| bad primes, index, units, Cl[2] | no rarity needed | inside H (Lemma H) |
   398	| exceptional zero | ≤ 1 class, every field | Lemma E, three tests |
   399	| characters miss | ≤ (log N)^{−2} per attempt | resample |
   400	| Wiedemann / finite-field roots | Las Vegas | retry |
   401	
   402	**N | f′(m) [proved].** Put m = g₀/g₁ mod N, so g₁m ≡ g₀.
   403	- f′ = F̂′ + g₁R + (g₁x − g₀)R′, so f′(m) ≡ f̂′(m) + g₁R_c(m) (mod N).
   404	- R_c(m) = Σ_i c_i m^{d−1−i} has coefficient 1 at c_{d−1}, and g₁ is a unit.
   405	- So for fixed c₀..c_{d−2}, at most one value of c_{d−1} ∈ [−H, H] (since 2H + 1 < N) gives N | f′(m). ∎
   406	
   407	**Lemma IR (irreducibility) [proved; elementary].** Fix g with gcd(g₁, N) = 1 and N free of primes ≤ B. For c
   408	uniform in C_H,
   409	  Pr[f reducible over Q] ≤ e·(1 − 1/(4d))^{|𝒫|},
   410	where 𝒫 is the set of primes in (r₀(d), ½ log H]. Hence the bound is ≤ exp(−log H/(13 d log log H)) for large N.
   411	*Proof.*
   412	- Let Q := ∏_{r∈𝒫} r ≤ H^{1/2+o(1)} ≤ 2H/d.
   413	- **Near-uniformity.** Each residue class of c mod Q has between ⌊(2H+1)/Q⌋^d and ⌈(2H+1)/Q⌉^d elements. So the law
   414	  of c mod Q is ≤ (1 + Q/(2H+1))^d ≤ e times uniform.
   415	- **The map mod r.** For r ∈ 𝒫 (r ∤ N since r < B), c ↦ f mod r is an affine bijection from F_r^d onto:
   416	  - if r ∤ g₁: all polynomials of degree ≤ d with f(m_r) = v_r := N g₁^{−d} ≠ 0, where m_r = g₀/g₁;
   417	  - if r | g₁: all polynomials with leading coefficient a_d ≢ 0 and arbitrary lower coefficients. Here
   418	    (g₁x − g₀) ≡ −g₀ is a unit.
   419	- **Counting irreducibles.**
   420	  - Monic irreducible Q of degree d with Q(0) = w ≠ 0 correspond to elements of F_{r^d} of degree d and norm
   421	    (−1)^d w. There are ≥ ((r^d − 1)/(r − 1) − 2r^{d/2})/d ≥ r^{d−1}/(2d) of them for r ≥ 16.
   422	  - Translating x ↦ x + m_r and summing over the r − 1 leading coefficients, the irreducible fraction of the affine
   423	    space is ≥ 1/(4d). In the case r | g₁ it is ≥ 1/(2d).
   424	- **Conclusion.** An irreducible reduction of degree d, with r ∤ f_d, forces f irreducible over Q. By CRT the
   425	  reductions are independent under the uniform law. Finally |𝒫| ≥ log H/(3 log log H). ∎
   426	- **Comparison.**
   427	  - pcest quoted LV's H^{−1/3+o(1)}, via a transfer of LV Lemma 5.1, checked at the level of statements. Codex
   428	    sketches the same order, O(H^{−1/3} log H), by a variance argument.
   429	  - Lemma IR is weaker but fully proved, and it is enough: it is L^{−ω(1)}, while D_k only needs Pr[𝒢ᶜ] ≤ γ/2.
   430	
   431	**What LV6 changes in pcest's theorems.**
   432	- **Nothing in FM, Theorem R, Lemma Kern, Prop 4.2 or Theorem 4.3.**
   433	- In Theorem A's assembly:
   434	  - (1) reject bad fields (table above); this halves γ at worst;
   435	  - (2) Lemma IR replaces the LV 5.1 transfer caveat;
   436	  - (3) the row count is M_rows = C_col + 12 with C_col ≤ (d+1)π(B) + O(log N);
   437	  - (4) FM's search uses batch smoothness (§6.2), and FM's count N_ℓ(A) is then recognized exactly, not with
   438	    probability 1 − N^{−2}.
