**Verdict [proved below, using cited results].** LV6 can be proved at fixed odd degree. One possible exceptional square class can be retained and eliminated by testing three dependencies. Complete-search batching also removes HECM without changing Theorem A’s exponent. Thus the appropriate headline remains **A, conditional on LD**, with degree-9 infimum \(0.9192\ldots\).

The report is also saved as [report.md](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/codex/scratch/lv6_1/report.md).

**Proposition LV6 [proved].** Write \(m=BL^{o(1)}\) for the number of rows. Assume the stated preprocessing, independent hidden-root draws, and an irreducible field polynomial \(f\) of fixed odd degree \(d\). Put
\[
t=f_d,\quad \beta=t\alpha,\quad
Q(T)=t^{d-1}f(T/t),\quad A=\mathbb Z[\beta],
\quad \delta=Q'(\beta).
\]
Suppose \(g_1,t,f'(g_0/g_1)\) are units modulo \(N\). Given certified pure descent rows, there is a Las Vegas algorithm producing a nonzero dependency and \(X^2\equiv Y^2\pmod N\) in expected \(m^{2+o(1)}\) bit operations, provided
\[
m\ge(d+1)\pi(B)+C\log N+4
\]
for a sufficiently large effective constant \(C\). The selected dependency and \(Y\) depend only on invariant data and independent coins. Consequently DD applies with fruitfulness exactly \(1-2^{1-\omega(N)}\).

The explicit surplus condition matters: the asymptotic row-count scale alone does not guarantee a kernel.

**Certificates and heights [proved].** For an atomic relation, write \(G(P)=\ell n_P\), with signed smooth \(n_P\), and set \(z_P=ta-b\beta\). Then
\[
\psi(z_P)=t\ell n_P/g_1,\qquad
\operatorname{Norm}(z_P)=t^{d-1}F(a,b).
\]
Substituting into the split gives
\[
r_i^2=q_i\psi(\Gamma_i),\qquad
\Gamma_i=\prod_Pz_P^{e_{iP}},\qquad
q_i=\prod_P(g_1/(tn_P))^{e_{iP}},
\]
where the signed atomic multiplicities total at most \(2k\).

In DD’s convention, take \(c_i=(q_i,\Gamma_i^{-1})\). Any additional invariant square multiplier is incorporated in \(W_i\).

Record the rational sign, prime exponents up to \(B\), and
\[
E_i=\sum_Pe_{iP}\pmod2.
\]
The last column handles **both \(g_1\) and \(f_d=t\)**: their rational exponents are \(E_i,-E_i\). Modulo two this is the number of atomic relations, counted with multiplicity. Neither integer needs factoring.

Here \(\|f\|_\infty=O_d(MH)=N^{O(1)}\) and \(V=N^{1/(4k)}L^{O(1)}\). Height enters through polynomial discriminant bounds, \(O(\log N)\) bits per atom, and \(O(m\log N)\) bits per product. No postprocessing step enumerates a box of side \(V\). LV Theorem 2.6 is therefore replaced by the following argument, rather than applied outside its growing-degree, \(L[1/3]\)-height hypotheses.

**Bad primes and residual classes [proved].** Set \(D=\operatorname{disc}Q\), and let \(S\) comprise prime ideals above rational primes dividing \(2tD\). For \(p\le B\), \(p\nmid tD\), record valuations at every simple root of \(Q\bmod p\), including zero. Primitivity implies that \(z_P\) can vanish only at its corresponding degree-one prime; its valuation is \(v_p(F(a,b))\). If \(p\mid b\), its norm is a unit.

All remaining algebraic kernel classes lie in
\[
H_S=\{x\in K^\times:v_{\mathfrak p}(x)\equiv0\pmod2
\text{ for }\mathfrak p\notin S\}/K^{\times2}.
\]
This handles nonintegral \(\alpha\) through the monic order \(A\). In particular,
\[
[O_K:A]^2\mid D,\qquad
D=t^{(d-1)(d-2)}\operatorname{disc}f,
\]
so \(\log|D|=O(\log N)\). Computing \(O_K\), its index, or the factorization of \(D\) is unnecessary.

Valuations at \(S\), followed by the ideal-square map, give
\[
|H_S|\le2^{|S|+d}|\operatorname{Cl}(K)[2]|
\le2^{|S|+d}h_K=N^{O(1)}.
\]
The everywhere-even kernel is an extension of class-group 2-torsion by units modulo squares. Dirichlet bounds the latter by \(2^d\); Minkowski bounds the class number, as also derived in [LV, Claim 6.5](https://arxiv.org/pdf/1805.08873). Neither obstruction is assumed trivial.

For nonzero \(h\in H_S\), let \(L_h=K(\sqrt h)\). Removing local square factors shows that the relative discriminant exponent is at most one at odd primes, and at most \(2v_{\mathfrak p}(2)+1\) at dyadic primes. Hence
\[
|D_{L_h}|\le |D_K|^2\,2^{3d}|tD|^d=N^{O(1)}.
\]
This avoids needing small element representatives for unit classes.

**Characters and exceptional zeros [proved using exact citations].** Apply [Lagarias–Odlyzko, Theorem 1.3, equations (1.6)–(1.7)](https://aareyanmanzoor.github.io/assets/articles/lagarias-odlyzko.pdf) to \(L_h/K\), with
\[
[L_h:\mathbb Q]=2d,\qquad
x=2^{\lceil\log_2N\rceil^4}.
\]
Its condition \(\log x\ge10(2d)(\log|D_{L_h}|)^2\) holds. Its error, including the exceptional-zero term, is \(o(x/\log x)\) unless
\[
(1-\beta_0)^{-1}>(\log N)^2.
\]

There is **at most one** exceptional nonzero class. [Stark, Lemma 8, p. 145](https://gdz.sub.uni-goettingen.de/download/pdf/PPN356556735_0023/PPN356556735_0023.pdf) transfers such a zero to a quadratic subfield \(F\subset L_h\), since its threshold \(4(2d)!\log|D_{L_h}|\) is \(O_d(\log N)\). Odd degree implies \(L_h=KF\). A degree-\(2d\) field cannot contain two quadratic subfields, which would generate a degree-four subfield. Distinct classes therefore give distinct \(F\). Their discriminants are \(N^{O(1)}\), so Landau–Page uniqueness—[LV, Fact 6.24](https://arxiv.org/pdf/1805.08873)—permits at most one zero this close to one. No ineffective Siegel lower bound is used.

Sample rational primes uniformly from \([x,2x]\), using rejection and deterministic primality certification. Factor \(Q\bmod p\), and choose uniformly one of \(d\) slots containing its linear factors, padded by trivial characters. A root \(s\) gives
\[
\chi_{p,s}(z_P)=\left(\frac{ta-bs}{p}\right).
\]
Degree-one prime ideals receive equal sampling mass; higher-degree primes contribute only \(O_d(\sqrt x)\). Chebotarev consequently gives detection probability at least \(1/(4d)\) for every nonexceptional nonzero class.

These primes exceed all atomic norms and \(2tD\), so evaluations are nonzero. Their bit lengths are polynomial in \(\log N\); sampling, factoring and evaluation cost \((\log N)^{O_d(1)}\).

On \(H_S\), use unramified quadratic Frobenius characters, normalizing even valuations by local squares. On these atomic products they equal the displayed residue symbols. With \(J=C\log N\),
\[
\Pr(\text{some nonexceptional nonzero class survives})
\le |H_S|(1-1/(4d))^J\le N^{-2}.
\]
Thus the surviving square-class kernel has dimension at most one, uniformly over every admissible field. LV’s random-field Lemma 6.10 is unnecessary.

**Linear algebra [cited/proved].** The matrix has \(O(m\log N)\) nonzero entries. Use [Kaltofen–Saunders 1991, Theorems 2–4 and §2](https://kaltofen.math.ncsu.edu/bibliography/91/KaSa91.pdf), the rigorous singular-system Wiedemann method, over an extension field of cardinality at least \(m^4\). Pad equations to make the matrix square. Their Toeplitz preconditioning preserves fast multiplication; work is \(O(m)\) matrix applications plus \(\widetilde O(m^2)\) field operations.

Expand extension-field kernel vectors in a binary basis, retaining two independent binary vectors; verify and retry. Their rank and uniform-solution guarantees give constant expected retries and \(m^{2+o(1)}\) bit cost.

For independent dependencies \(s_1,s_2\), one of
\[
s_1,\quad s_2,\quad s_1+s_2
\]
has trivial algebraic square class. Test these three directly.

**Square roots and evaluation [proved].** For a tested product \(\Gamma\), choose \(D_0\), a product of norms of inverted atoms, clearing denominators. Then \(Z=D_0^2\Gamma\in A\). If \(\eta^2=Z\), then \(\eta\in O_K\) and \(\delta\eta\in A\): the trace-dual identity is \(A^*=\delta^{-1}A\), and \(O_K\subset A^*\).

An explicit coefficient bound follows as follows. Put
\[
R_0=1+\|Q\|_\infty,\quad
Z_0=1+\max_P(|ta|+|b|R_0),\quad n_0\le2km.
