Each conjugate of an atom is at most \(Z_0\), its inverse at most \(Z_0^{d-1}\), and \(|D_0|\le Z_0^{dn_0}\). With
\(C_\delta=1+d^2\|Q\|_\infty R_0^{d-1}\), each coefficient of \(w=\delta\eta\) is bounded by
\[
C_*=d!R_0^{d^2}C_\delta Z_0^{2dn_0}.
\]
Indeed, invert the embedding Vandermonde matrix using cofactors; its determinant has absolute value \(\sqrt{|D|}\ge1\). Thus both \(U=\delta^2Z\) and the desired \(w\) have \(T=O(m\log N)\) bits.

At a sampled prime, \(Q\) is squarefree and \(U\) is a unit. Enumerate its at most \(2^d\) roots in the finite algebra, then Newton–Hensel lift every branch using
\[
w\longmapsto(w+U/w)/2.
\]
Double precision until the modulus exceeds \(2C_*\), center coefficients, and verify \(w^2=U\) exactly. A global square supplies a branch; no verified branch means nonsquare. Fixed degree makes \(2^d\) constant. Balanced products and fast arithmetic cost \(\widetilde O_d(T)(\log N)^{O(1)}\).

For a verified branch,
\[
\psi(\sqrt\Gamma)=
\frac{w(tg_0/g_1)}{D_0Q'(tg_0/g_1)}\pmod N.
\]
The denominator is a unit: \(D_0\) uses \(t\) and smooth norms, while
\(Q'(tg_0/g_1)=t^{d-2}f'(g_0/g_1)\).

Halving the recorded rational exponents computes the rational square root, using inverses for negative exponents. This requires no root-extraction oracle modulo \(N\). Its product with the displayed algebraic image is \(Y\); set \(X=\prod r_iW_i\).

All retries, dependencies, branch choices and verification use invariant certificates and independent coins. Select the first verified dependency before examining hidden roots or a final gcd. This proves (T). Following a trivial gcd, restart with fresh hidden roots.

**Exceptional events [proved bounds].** Let \(\mu=\mathbb E q_A=L^{-o(1)}\). Keeping amplifies a field-event probability by at most \(1/\mu\).

| Event | Bound and treatment |
|---|---|
| \(g_1\) nonunit | Proper factor immediately, since \(0<g_1<N\). |
| Degree drop | Probability zero thereafter: \(t\equiv a_d\not\equiv0\pmod{g_1}\). |
| Reducible \(f\) | \(O_d(H^{-1/3}\log H)\); after keeping, \(N^{-h/3+o(1)}\). Reject. |
| Nonprimitive \(f\) | Content divides \(N\); proper content gives a factor. Whole-\(N\) content is covered below. |
| \(t\) or \(f'(g_0/g_1)\) nonunit | Proper gcd is success. Whole-\(N\) rejection probability at most \(2/N+2/(2H+1)\), then divide by \(\mu\). |
| Zero \(F(P)\) or \(G(P)\) | Excluded by candidate filters. |
| Nonunit accepted \(G(P)\) | Its special prime divides \(N\), giving a proper factor. |
| Zero discriminant | Impossible for irreducible \(f\) in characteristic zero. |
| Index, bad primes, units, 2-torsion | No rarity assumption; included in \(H_S\). |
| Exceptional zero | No rarity assumption; at most one class, handled above. |
| Character miss | At most \(N^{-2}\) for every admissible field; retry if no square verifies. |
| Solver/finite-field randomization | Verified outputs; constant expected retries; no incorrect outputs. |
| Final trivial gcd | Exactly \(2^{1-\omega(N)}\); fresh-root restart. |

For reducibility, fix \(g\), omit primes dividing \(g_1N\), and take \(p\asymp H^{1/3}\). The coefficient box modulo \(p\), respectively \(pq\), differs from uniform by \(O_d(p/H)\), respectively \(O_d(pq/H)\). The affine family contains all polynomials with a prescribed nonzero value. Counting norm fibres in \(\mathbb F_{p^d}^\times\), subtracting proper subfields, and summing over leading coefficients gives an irreducible fraction at least \(1/(2d)\). The variance bound is
\(O(1/\pi(H^{1/3})+H^{-1/3})\).
A reducible polynomial has no degree-\(d\) irreducible reduction.

The whole-\(N\) bounds follow because \(t\) and \(f'(g_0/g_1)\) are affine in separate random coefficients, with unit coefficient \(g_1\) modulo \(N\).

**Consequently [proved], FM, R, Kern and Theorem 4.3 need no alteration.** Rejected fields have polynomially small probability, negligible against their \(L^{O(1)}\) candidate bounds and after amplification. Only assembly bookkeeping and search implementation change.

**HECM verdict.**

**(i) [Cited/proved completion.]** [LPP I, Theorem 1.3, p. 399](https://math.dartmouth.edu/~carlp/hyperI.pdf), checked against the page image, states: for \(c_1=1980000^{1/3}\), effective \(x\ge x_3\), \(z=L_x[2/3,c_1]\), and \(x^{3/5}\le y\le x\), the number of \(z\)-smooth integers in \([x,x+y]\) is at least
\[
y\exp[-(\log x)^{1/3}(\log\log x)^{8/3}].
\]
Take \(x=p^2-p^{3/2}/2\), \(y=p^{3/2}\). The count is \(p^{3/2}L_p[2/3,-o(1)]\gg p\). [LPP II, Theorem 1.1](https://math.dartmouth.edu/~carlp/PDF/paper129.pdf) supplies, for \(p>8100\), the quintic fibre lower bound with at most \(p\) exceptional orders.

Genus2rh Propositions A/B and Lemmas 1–4 then give
\[
\Pr(\text{factor per curve})\ge
\frac{(S_w(p)-p)(1-4/\sqrt p)}
{24000\cdot144000\,p^{3/2}\lambda(p)\lambda(q)},
\]
where \(S_w(p)\) counts smooth orders in the stated interval and
